          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get migraines maybe six times a month.",
          "answer": "Your description didn't match any emergency pattern in the checks I ran. That isn't a clearance, so if your headaches become severe, change in pattern, or come with new symptoms, seek medical advice.\n\nThe national headache society guidance (migraine: acute treatment, when prevention starts, and medication-overuse limits) makes one point especially clear. Taking pain medicine too often can keep headaches frequent. The guidance gives these limits:\n\n- Triptans, ergots, opioids and combination pain relievers: fewer than 10 days per month.\n- Simple pain relievers: fewer than 15 days per month.\n\nIt describes this \"medication-overuse headache\" as common and reversible, and says it is checked before a preventive treatment is increased. Keeping a simple count of the days you take medicine for headaches may help you and a clinician see the pattern.\n\nThe same guidance says that when prevention should start, and which treatments to use, are decisions for a clinician. It also says doses are outside its scope. The passages I retrieved don't cover prevention options, when to start them, or what a headache diary should contain, so a clinician or pharmacist is the right person for those questions.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
