          "session_id": "full-mt-m-003",
          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is that enough to need something preventive?",
          "answer": "The national headache society guidance says preventive treatment is usually offered when attacks are frequent or disabling enough that treatment during attacks alone is not enough. It gives this threshold: four or more migraine days per month with some disability, or fewer attacks where the disability is severe. Six a month is above the frequency mark, so the question turns on disability. Does it stop you from working, studying, or doing daily tasks?\n\nThe guidance also says the aim of prevention is fewer and less severe attacks, not none. A 50 percent cut in migraine days is the usual sign that a treatment is working.\n\nThe passages I retrieved don't say which preventive treatments exist or how to choose one. That decision belongs with a clinician, who can weigh your attack pattern, your medicine use, and your other health conditions. It would help to bring a count of your headache days and a note of how much each one affects your day.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
